//@ expect: reject
//@ reason: `ref` may not appear inside a container's element type. `std::vector<ref
//@ reason: Node>` would let an observer's lifetime outlive its owner while stored
//@ reason: anonymously inside a reallocatable container -- precise per-element
//@ reason: provenance tracking would be needed to make this safe, and CeeLing takes
//@ reason: the cheaper escape-by-construction route instead (same design as the
//@ reason: observer-parameter ban): this is a parse error, not a semantic reject.
//
// Container-held observers (the design notes Phase 2.6 slice 2 follow-up) are forbidden by
// construction, not by analysis: `generic_type`'s single type argument is `$.type`,
// and `observer_qualifier` (`ref`) is only reachable from `variable_declaration` and
// `parameter_list` in the grammar -- it can never appear inside a `<...>` type
// argument, a member-variable declaration, or anywhere else `$.type` is used. So
// `std::vector<ref Node>` fails to parse; there is no dedicated checker rule for this
// because there is nothing for a checker to see -- the syntax itself does not exist.

class Node {
public:
    int v;
    Node(int x) { v = x; }
};

int main() {
    std::vector<ref Node> v;   // ERROR: `ref` cannot appear inside a type argument
    return 0;
}
